datasail_cluster_1d.py is DataSAIL's cluster-based cold split (their "C1"):

  https://github.com/kalininalab/DataSAIL
  commit  c31695688a9fd59c2fd17314fe27e36ddc701c3a
  path    datasail/solver/cluster_1d.py
  sha256  0802b3774fbebdfb70fd26cafff7470c7a512876ff3bc9bb4e1be97d270cfb07
  licence MIT, see ../../licenses/DATASAIL-MIT-LICENSE

Roman Joeres, David B. Blumenthal, Olga V. Kalinina, "DataSAIL: Data Splitting Against Information Leakage". https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.11.15.566305

Four edits, all recorded here and nowhere else:

  1. the import of solve / compute_limits / stratification_constraints now points at datasail_support.py beside it. Importing the installed datasail would pull in its whole clustering stack (grakel, h5py, sklearn, cvxopt, matplotlib) to reach three functions, and its solve() sets a time limit and fourteen threads, which rules.md's "one shot" forbids.

  2. the objective is summed over a `pairs` argument -- the cluster pairs that have a similarity -- instead of over all of them. Every term left out is multiplied by a similarity of zero, so the objective is the same number; built over all pairs it would be 1.18 million cvxpy atoms where 1,931 matter.

  3. the loss is built as one cvxpy atom over all the pairs at once rather than one atom per pair. cvxpy warns about exactly this ("Objective contains too many subexpressions. Consider vectorizing"), and with 5,239 pairs its canonicalisation ran twelve minutes without finishing, for a model SCIP then solves in seconds. max over the splits of x[s,e1] - x[s,e2] is 1 when a pair is separated and 0 when it is not, term for term the same as the loop.

  4. an optional `floors` argument: (weights, one floor per split) pairs, each added as constraint (17) again over a second weight vector. C1 takes ONE weight vector, so a benchmark weighted by its proteins had no way to keep rows in test as well -- fireprot's test set came out at 9 proteins and 72 rows, too few to score. split.py passes the rows as the second vector with a floor on every split (train 60%, val and test 5%). Left out, as it is for every benchmark weighted by rows, the model is exactly theirs.

The model itself -- one binary per (split, cluster), every cluster in exactly one split, a weight floor per split, and the weighted cross-split similarity as the loss -- is otherwise untouched. That is the method.

