DataSAIL's double-cold split (their "C2") is NOT used and not vendored:

  https://github.com/kalininalab/DataSAIL
  commit  c31695688a9fd59c2fd17314fe27e36ddc701c3a
  path    datasail/solver/cluster_2d.py
  sha256  506a6316390d7a3b97dddd0d8f1a0db732d1256fa99122a5e157a0f1536b2950

It was run on bindingdb and lp-pdbbind so their molecules would be split by the same model as their proteins, and it cannot hold the band there: it assigns the two axes independently at 58.6/20.7/20.7, and ONE molecule cluster carries 83.8% of bindingdb's row weight (lp-pdbbind 68.5%, and its protein axis 59.4%, already past the 58.6% train target). A cluster that big lands whole in one part, so validation and test cannot both reach their floors at any epsilon or cluster count -- bindingdb came out 99.4/0.2/0.4 and lp-pdbbind 93.2/3.6/3.1. DataSAIL's own PDBBind example runs on the core set, 182 entities, where no such blob exists, and their paper says only that assigning every interaction "cannot be guaranteed", not that the survivors land on the asked-for ratios. Those molecules follow their rows instead and the chemistry rule is paid for in dropped rows, in split.py.
